+## Postscript (v0.74.1, 2026-07-30): per-shim pending.json + 9.6 cleanup glob amend
+
+v0.74.1 amend: two follow-on decisions that build on this ADR.
+
+### §Post.1 Why 5 unique `FluxingStage2ShimSwap_<shim>` task names (was: 1 shared)
+
+v0.74 used a single shared `pending.json` (one file appended by all 5
+stage blocks, consumed by one `FluxingStage2ShimSwap` task at boot).
+With 5 stage blocks all writing to the same file and 1 boot-time task
+consuming it, there was a race when multiple boot-time tasks ran
+concurrently (rare, but possible on multi-shim installs across reboots).
+More importantly, the batch file's self-cleanup (`del pending.json`) at
+the end would delete entries still being processed by another concurrent
+batch run.
+
+v0.74.1 decision: **5 unique task names** (`FluxingStage2ShimSwap_<shim>`
+for `weasel`, `weaselx64`, `server`, `deployer`, `setup`), each with
+its own per-shim pending file (`%TEMP%\fluxing-stage2-shim\<shim>.json`)
+and per-shim done file (`<shim>.done.json`). Each At-startup task
+runs independently with SHIM env var passed via `install.nsi` `/TR`
+`"cmd /c set SHIM=<shim> && ..."`. The 5 concurrent boot-time tasks
+no longer race on a shared file — each reads/writes/deletes only its
+own file. Test 7 of `Test-Stage2ShimInstall.ps1` validates the 5 unique
+names + per-shim FileOpen paths + SHIM env var prefixes at source
+level (commit-time evidence per FR-003).
+
+**Consequences**:
+
+- **Easier**: 5 boot-time tasks are now genuinely independent. A
+  partial failure in one shim's batch run does not affect the other
+  4. Per-shim `done.json` + `Stage2Done_<shim>` registry markers
+  give granular forensic visibility into which shim succeeded vs
+  failed (vs the v0.74 single marker that hid partial failures).
+- **Harder**: 6 `schtasks /Delete` defensive cleanup commands on
+  every install (was: 0). Install-boundary Delete guards grew from
+  1 file to 5 (`IfFileExists "<shim>.json" 0 +2 / Delete "<shim>.json"`
+  for each shim). Stage 2 batch file grew from `pending.json` →
+  `%SHIM%.json` path resolution at the top.
+- **Risks**: A buggy `set SHIM=<shim>` prefix in `/TR` would silently
+  skip the boot-time apply (env var empty → batch exits with code 2 →
+  no swap). Mitigation: Test 7 anchors check for the SHIM prefix in
+  each `/TR`; any regression fails commit-time ship-gate.
+
+### §Post.2 Why 9.6 cleanup glob instead of exact-match (L##-PhaseL-9.7 amend)
+
+v0.74 cleanup at `install.nsi:240-247` + `1078-1085` used exact-match
+`Delete "$WINDIR\System32\weaselx64.dll"` + `Delete "$WINDIR\System32\weasel.dll"`.
+This was sufficient for the v0.74 era, but earlier versions (v0.66,
+v0.67, v0.68) had left 11 backup-pattern legacy files in `System32`
+(`.old`, `.bak`, `.v465`, `.v466bak-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS`, `.ime` variants)
+that the v0.74 exact-match cleanup missed. The Windows shell loader
+and CTF subsystem then repeatedly retried the locked backup files
+when the user switched IMEs, causing the perceived "17-minute freeze"
+(see `feedback_l9_install_freeze_ux_timing.md` for the UX timing
+analysis).
+
+L##-PhaseL-9.7 forensics also discovered a hidden bug: the cleanup
+helper used `${GetSize} "S"` which returns *sectors*, not bytes. The
+`$R3 >= 100000` size threshold then meant "≥ 50 MB", silently skipping
+all DLLs under 50 MB (including the 1.16 MB `weaselx64.dll`). Combined,
+the cleanup was a complete silent no-op despite appearing to pass
+audit at `output/install.nsi:240-247`.
+
+v0.74.1 decision: **9.6 cleanup glob** with `FindFirst $WINDIR\System32\weasel*.dll`
++ `loop_weasel_dll:` + `${GetSize} "" $R2 $R3` (returns bytes, not
+sectors) + `$R3 >= 102400` (100 KB threshold) + `Delete /REBOOTOK` +
+mirror in `Section Uninstall`. Defense-in-depth: exact-match fallback
+for `weaselx64.dll` + `weasel.dll` is preserved before the glob loop
+in case `FindFirst` is unavailable on legacy Windows.
+
+**Consequences**:
+
+- **Easier**: 11 backup-pattern legacy files are now removed
+  automatically on every install + uninstall. Per-machine verification
+  via `_check_system32_cleanup.ps1 -Mode Audit` (8/8 anchors PASS) +
+  `-Mode PretouchUninstall` (creates 11 fake files in System32 for
+  end-to-end verification).
+- **Harder**: NSIS `CleanupLegacySystem32Weasel` helper function is
+  ~70 lines longer than the v0.74 exact-match version. Per `simplify`
+  skill review, the helper could be further DRY'd but the verbose
+  FindFirst pattern is preferred for debuggability.
+- **Risks**: A bug in `${GetSize}` units (sectors vs bytes) would
+  re-introduce L##-PhaseL-9.7 silently. Mitigation: 3 anchors in
+  `_check_system32_cleanup.ps1` `Audit` mode explicitly check
+  `${GetSize} "" $R2 $R3` (bytes) + `102400` (100 KB). Any change to
+  these patterns fails ship-gate.
+
+### §Post.3 Why emoji.json defense-in-depth (L##-PhaseL-9.8 amend)
+
+Not directly part of this ADR (shim swap only), but related to the
+9.6 cleanup pattern. v0.74 shipped `data\opencc\*.json` to
+`$INSTDIR\data\opencc\`, but the libRime OpenCC loader was reading
+from `$INSTDIR\weasel\data\opencc\`. The fallback "config not found"
+path was silently triggered, leaving emoji conversion disabled without
+a visible error. L##-PhaseL-9.8 added a defense-in-depth secondary
+install to the correct path so both locations are populated.
+
+**Consequences**: OpenCC loader no longer falls back. Both
+`$INSTDIR\data\opencc\` and `$INSTDIR\weasel\data\opencc\` are
+populated. Real-machine verification via `TestOpenccDataPath`
+(`Find /C "opencc config not found" rime.weasel.INFO.*.log` = 0).
+
